    Recycle completed device-local temporaries for the zeroed API
    
    Device-local buffers allocated by `GpuBuffer::zeroed` were destroyed on every
    step. Retain bounded, already-completed allocations in the submission arena and
    hand them back only to the zeroed constructor, which re-establishes its zero
    guarantee before the caller can observe the bytes. Other constructors and
    host-visible upload chunks keep their existing paths, so uninitialised
    allocations never receive recycled contents.
    
    Drops take the arena lock without blocking, because a retirement reaped while
    that lock is held must not re-enter it, and teardown drains the pool after the
    lock is released. Retention is capped at 4 MiB per allocation and 256 MiB in
    total.
    
    The new test poisons a released allocation with 7.0, then asserts the next
    zeroed request reuses the same buffer and reads as zero. Performance effect is
    not yet qualified by the release protocol.
    
